body:
## Goal

Build a small, safe C++ wrapper around lexbor's HTML parser, so future code in the renderer can parse HTML without touching lexbor's raw C API directly.

This is Phase 1 of the return-to-ADR-001 plan (see ADR-006). It builds on Phase 0 (#90), which vendored lexbor and proved it links.

## Where the Code Lives

```
src/renderer/html/
├── document.hpp    — public interface
└── document.cpp    — implementation
```

`src/renderer/` is new. It will grow to hold `dom/`, `layout/`, and `paint/` in later phases. `html/` is the parser sub-component.

Add `renderer/html/document.cpp` to the `honeybee_lib` target in `src/CMakeLists.txt` (same target as `base/` and `net/` — no GTK dependency, fully testable).

## The Interface

```cpp
// src/renderer/html/document.hpp
#pragma once

#include <memory>
#include <string>
#include <string_view>

// Forward-declare the lexbor type so this header doesn't leak lexbor into
// every translation unit that includes it.
struct lxb_html_document;

namespace honeybee::renderer::html {

class Document {
public:
    // Parse an HTML string. Returns nullptr on unrecoverable failure.
    // lexbor handles malformed HTML gracefully (per the WHATWG spec), so
    // this will succeed for most real-world input, including broken markup.
    static std::unique_ptr<Document> parse(std::string_view html);

    ~Document();

    // Move-only: the underlying lexbor document has a single owner.
    Document(Document&&) noexcept;
    Document& operator=(Document&&) noexcept;
    Document(const Document&) = delete;
    Document& operator=(const Document&) = delete;

    // Text content of the <title> element. Empty string if no title.
    std::string title() const;

    // Concatenated text of all visible text nodes in document order.
    // Whitespace is preserved as-is for now; normalization is a later concern.
    std::string text_content() const;

private:
    explicit Document(lxb_html_document* doc) noexcept;

    lxb_html_document* doc_ = nullptr;
};

} // namespace honeybee::renderer::html
```

Design choices to note:

- **`parse()` returns `unique_ptr<Document>`** — the Document owns the lexbor tree for its lifetime. Move-only, not copyable.
- **`lxb_html_document` is forward-declared**, not included. Callers don't need lexbor headers.
- **No exceptions** — parse failure returns `nullptr`, matching the project's existing style (see `base/url.hpp` and `net/response.hpp`).
- **Namespace** `honeybee::renderer::html` — mirrors the directory layout.

## Implementation Notes

- `parse()` uses `lxb_html_document_create()` + `lxb_html_document_parse()`. The destructor calls `lxb_html_document_destroy()`.
- `title()` walks the tree for the `<title>` element (there's only ever one in a valid document; lexbor's parser enforces this) and collects its text.
- `text_content()` recursively walks all text nodes. Reuse the walker pattern from `tests/test_lexbor_smoke.cpp`.
- Consider a small internal helper `collect_text(lxb_dom_node_t*)` — the smoke test already has a version we can lift.

## Tests

Add `tests/test_html_document.cpp` and register it in `tests/CMakeLists.txt`. Cover:

1. **Valid HTML** — `<html><head><title>Hi</title></head><body><p>Hello</p></body></html>` → `title() == "Hi"`, `text_content()` contains `"Hello"`.
2. **Missing `<title>`** — `<html><body><p>Hi</p></body></html>` → `title() == ""`.
3. **Malformed HTML** — `<html><body><p>Unclosed` → parses successfully, `text_content()` contains `"Unclosed"`.
4. **Empty input** — `""` → parses successfully (lexbor produces a minimal document).
5. **UTF-8 content** — `<p>مرحبا</p>` → `text_content()` contains the Arabic string intact.
6. **Move semantics** — construct via `parse()`, `std::move` into another variable, verify the new owner works and the original is null.
7. **Nested elements** — `<div><p>A</p><p>B</p></div>` → text content is `"AB"` (or `"A B"` with whitespace — decide and document).

## Acceptance Criteria

- `src/renderer/html/document.{hpp,cpp}` exists with the interface above.
- All 7+ tests pass.
- Existing tests (including the Phase 0 smoke test) still pass.
- No GTK dependency in `honeybee_lib` — this code is pure and testable.
- CI passes.

## Out of Scope

- DOM node classes (that's Phase 3).
- CSS parsing (lexbor has a CSS module we'll wire up later).
- Integration with `HoneyBeeWindow` (that's Phase 2).
- Element traversal / querying (Phase 3+).

## Learning Outcomes

- RAII wrapping a C library that uses create/destroy lifecycle.
- Forward-declaring a C struct to avoid leaking headers.
- Move-only types in C++ (`unique_ptr`, deleted copy, defaulted move).
- Recursive DOM traversal in lexbor's tree representation.

## Dependencies

- Blocked by: nothing (Phase 0 merged in #91).
- Blocks: Phase 2 (window integration) and Phase 3 (DOM layer).
